    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded a read-time derived `timezone` field on the owner response, mapping region \u2192 IANA name via a fixed table (NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane).\n\nThree changes, mirroring how `locality` already works:\n\n1. **`model/OwnerRegion.java`** \u2014 added the fixed `REGION_TIMEZONE` table and a `timezoneOf(region)` lookup (returns `null` for regions with no mapping, e.g. `UNKNOWN`).\n\n2. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added `@Mapping(target = \"timezone\", ...)` plus a `@Named(\"timezone\")` method that derives the region exactly as `locality(owner)` does, then maps it through `OwnerRegion.timezoneOf(...)`. Reusing `locality(owner)` means the timezone honors the same customerCode/postcode/city resolution precedence already established for the region.\n\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `timezone` string property to the `Owner` schema (after `locality`) so the generated `OwnerDto` gains `getTimezone()/setTimezone()`.\n\nNo entity, YAML route, or schema/DB change was needed \u2014 like `locality`, `timezone` is purely derived at read time and flows through the existing mapper used by every owner responder.\n\nThe target test `coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion` and the full suite pass (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
