    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is clean and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **Table in the right unit.** `REGION_TIMEZONE` sits in `OwnerRegion`, alongside the existing `CITY_REGION` and `REGION_POSTCODES` tables \u2014 the class whose stated role is being the \"single source of truth for the fixed ... tables.\" A region-to-timezone table is exactly that kind of domain fact, so it belongs there and nowhere else. `timezoneOf(region)` is placed right next to `regionOf`, mirroring the existing accessor pattern (map lookup, null/`UNKNOWN` documented).\n\n- **No duplication of the region derivation.** The mapper's `timezone(owner)` delegates to `locality(owner)` to get the region rather than re-deriving it from city/postcode/customerCode. This keeps the \"what counts as the owner's region\" logic in one place and automatically tracks any future change to `locality`, which is the correct dependency direction and matches the spec's \"derived from the locality/region.\"\n\n- **Mapper method parallels its siblings.** `@Named(\"timezone\")` + `@Mapping` entry follow the identical shape of `locality`, `contactPreference`, etc. Nothing in `OwnerMapper` or `OwnerRegion` is becoming a catch-all; each gained one focused member.\n\n- **OpenAPI doc is consistent** with the neighboring `locality` field (readOnly, derived-at-read-time description, example).\n\nA maintainer here would find this natural \u2014 it's the same pattern the surrounding code already uses.",
